  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThere has been very little change in the weather over the past 24 \nhours as a slow moving surface low spins over western Wisconsin. In \nfact, the PWAT value on the 03/00z, 03/12z, and 04/00z MPX sounding \nhas been holding steady at 1.34\". This is well over 200% of normal \nfor this time of year. Both MSP and STC set new daily rainfall \nrecords for November 3rd, with STC already exceeding the monthly \naverage precip in just the past two days (1.82\" over the past two \ndays, monthly average is 1.37\"). MSP isn't far behind the average \nNovember monthly precip of 1.61\" with 1.31\" just in these first few \ndays of the month. An additional half inch to an inch of rain is \nexpected area wide through tonight before this system finally moves \nout to the east. There may be a brief lull in the rain for \neastern portions of the forecast area as the precip shield \npivots and eventually slides eastward again this evening. Some \nscattered showers are still expected across far eastern \nMinnesota and western Minnesota to start the day tomorrow, with \ndrier conditions settling by the afternoon. CAA on the backside \nof the low and clearing skies overnight will finally allow \ntemperatures to move more than 10 degrees between the daily high\nand low. \n\nFriday into Saturday will feature a brief cool down with the Twin \nCities finally looking at its first 32 degree morning of the season \non Saturday. This would be top three latest freeze on record for \nMSP, with November 18th 2016 likely safe in the top spot. Outside of \nthe metro, temperatures will fall into the 20s. Highs recover into \nthe upper 40s to low 50s with ample sunshine through Sunday. This \nwarm up continues into early next week with a weak, moisture starved \nshortwave moving through late Monday. Dry conditions are expected to \ncontinue through the remainder of the forecast period with high \npressure in place.",
